// system/posix — raw POSIX syscalls.

module "system/posix"

import "std/target"

/// Standard output's file descriptor.
export STDOUT :: 1

/// Standard error's file descriptor.
export STDERR :: 2

/// `write(2)`. Byte count written, or -1 with `errno` set.
@extern("write")
export sys_write :: fn(fd: i32, buf: u8[], count: usize) -> i32

// ---- clocks: BLOCKED, and not by what I first said -------------------------
//
// `clock_gettime` cannot be declared here. `<time.h>` is one of the seven
// headers already visible in an emitted unit (via `vader.h`), and it declares
//
//     int clock_gettime(clockid_t, struct timespec *);
//
// against which our synthesised `int32_t clock_gettime(int32_t, void*)` is a
// conflicting declaration — the C compiler refuses outright. Nothing about the
// LAYOUT is the obstacle: a two-element `i64[]` does stand in for `struct
// timespec` on every 64-bit target we build for (verified: sizeof 16, tv_sec@0,
// tv_nsec@8).
//
// The alternatives do not help. `gettimeofday` lives in `<sys/time.h>`, which
// is NOT visible, so it would link — but `struct timeval` holds a 32-bit
// `suseconds_t` on Darwin and a 64-bit one on Linux, so one cell shape cannot
// serve both. `time(NULL)` is in `<time.h>` too, and gives seconds.
//
// This is the `@include` case, not the `@c_layout` case: the fix is to stop
// synthesising a prototype the header already provides.
